\ Dogfood: Slice 5 fixed-size arrays + usize, and Slice 6 references. A
\ bounded `i64` stack over an embedded array and a runtime `usize` cursor,
\ exercising array-as-field, the runtime cursor (the bounds-trap path),
\ in-place mutation through `&!>`, a non-consuming read through `&>`/`@`, and
\ `len`.
\
\ `pop`/`peek` bundle their two results (the updated `Stack` and the read
\ value) into a `Popped` struct: a user-defined word may only return one
\ value through a call, so the pair rides home as a single aggregate and the
\ caller destructures it with the generated `Popped>` word.

type: Stack 
  items [i64 16] 
  top usize ;

type: Popped 
  rest Stack 
  item i64 ;

: empty ( -- Stack )
  0 16 fill 0 >usize Stack ;

: push ( Stack i64 -- Stack )
  | s x |
  s Stack>top | i |
  &!s &!Stack>items i &!> x !   \ write in place, no whole-array rebuild
  s s Stack>top 1 + Stack<top ;

: pop ( Stack -- Popped )
  | s |
  s Stack>top 1 - | i |
  &s &Stack>items i &> @ | v |  \ read without aliasing the array
  s i Stack<top v Popped ;

: peek ( Stack -- Popped )
  | s |
  s Stack>top 1 - | i |
  &s &Stack>items i &> @ | v |
  s v Popped ;

: main ( -- )
  empty
  1 push 2 push 3 push
  peek Popped> .                \ 3, non-consuming: stack unchanged underneath
  pop Popped> .                 \ 3
  pop Popped> .                 \ 2
  peek Popped> .                \ 1, one item left
  Stack>items len .             \ 16, len is a compile-time constant of the array shape
  drop ;                        \ discard the residual items array
